/* Detailed lecture learning: L67 grounded in uploaded professor slides (43 pages).
   Paraphrased teaching notes; original lecture images are not reproduced. */
window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN = window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN || {};
window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN['67']={
title:'Nervous System: Normal Physiology and Response to Injury',
source:'Instructor Lecture 67 · 43 slides · learning objectives slide 2',
sections:[
{title:'Objectives and orientation',slides:'1–5',points:[
['Instructor expectations','Identify normal brain and spinal cord, gray versus white matter, nervous-system cell types, CSF flow, meninges, cellular injury, neuronal necrosis, Wallerian degeneration, edema, infection routes, and the blood-brain barrier.'],
['Nervous-system divisions','CNS = brain and spinal cord. PNS = sensory and motor divisions; motor pathways include somatic and autonomic, with sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric components.'],
['Brain regions','Cerebrum processes cognition and sensation and initiates movement; cerebellum coordinates proprioception and motor function; brainstem regulates essential autonomic functions and relays tracts; spinal cord connects brain and body.']
]},
{title:'Gray versus white matter; normal gross recognition',slides:'6–12',points:[
['Gray matter','Numerous neuronal cell bodies, dendrites, axons and supporting glia; forms cerebral and cerebellar cortex and basal nuclei. In spinal cord, gray matter is central.'],
['White matter','Predominantly myelinated axonal tracts with oligodendrocytes, astrocytes and microglia; lies deeper in the cerebrum and cerebellum but peripheral in spinal cord.'],
['Sulci, gyri and ventricles','Gyri are raised cortical folds, sulci are intervening grooves; the lateral ventricles contain CSF. Normal gross anatomy should be learned across species.'],
['Professor’s diagnostic vocabulary','In every brain image describe symmetry versus asymmetry AND unilateral versus bilateral distribution before proposing a diagnosis.'],
['Why distribution matters','Symmetric lesions may support systemic metabolic/toxic injury; focal/asymmetric lesions raise localized vascular, inflammatory, traumatic or neoplastic processes. Distribution alone does not prove cause.']
]},
{title:'Neurons and astrocytes',slides:'13–15',points:[
['Neuron compartments','Dendrites receive inputs, soma processes and maintains cellular homeostasis, and axons transmit electrical impulses to synapses or neuromuscular junctions.'],
['Neuronal energy vulnerability','Neural tissue has exceptionally high metabolic demand; continuous oxygen and glucose are needed for ATP-dependent membrane pumps.'],
['Astrocyte homeostasis','Astrocytes regulate extracellular ions and water, neurotransmitters and metabolites; contribute to sequestration/metabolism of ammonia and heavy metals and participate in CNS immune signaling.'],
['Astrocytes in repair','They hypertrophy and extend/proliferate processes, secrete support factors and create a loose glial scar around injury. This is weaker and less complete than fibroblast-rich capsules elsewhere.'],
['Which cell does what?','Neurons are most vulnerable to hypoxic ischemia. Astrocytes are key for glial scarring, while microglia/macrophages specialize in cleanup.']
]},
{title:'Oligodendrocytes and microglia',slides:'16–17',points:[
['Oligodendrocyte function','Builds CNS myelin around axonal internodes. Nodes of Ranvier permit saltatory conduction, increasing the speed of signal transmission.'],
['Demyelination consequences','Loss of functional myelin slows or disrupts conduction, potentially leading to ataxia and proprioceptive abnormalities; canine distemper is a lecture example.'],
['Microglia','Resident macrophage-lineage immune sentinel cells involved in surveillance, immune regulation and phagocytosis; large proportions of macrophages in some CNS injuries are recruited from blood monocytes.'],
['Mechanism connection','Demyelination is not the same as axon loss. Microglial activation and phagocytosis can accompany either lesion.']
]},
{title:'Ependyma, ventricles, and CSF circulation',slides:'18–20',points:[
['Ependymal cells','Ciliated cuboidal-to-columnar cells line the ventricles, mesencephalic aqueduct and spinal central canal; support circulation and material exchange with CSF.'],
['Ventricular components','Two lateral ventricles, one third ventricle, and one fourth ventricle, continuous with the spinal central canal.'],
['CSF production and function','Primarily formed at choroid plexuses; cushions CNS and participates in nutrient and waste exchange. The lecture associates CSF production with specialized ependymal epithelium.'],
['Memorize flow','Lateral ventricles → third ventricle → mesencephalic aqueduct → fourth ventricle → lateral apertures → subarachnoid space → arachnoid villi for venous reabsorption.'],
['Why it matters','Obstruction along CSF flow can enlarge upstream ventricles, creating pressure injury. This sets up the hydrocephalus lecture.']
]},
{title:'Meninges and supporting vascular structures',slides:'21–23',points:[
['Dura mater','Outermost, dense collagenous protective meninx; cranial and spinal relationships differ.'],
['Arachnoid and pia','Arachnoid is the middle meninx; pia intimately coats the CNS. Together they are leptomeninges; the CSF- and vessel-filled subarachnoid space lies between them.'],
['Glia limitans','Astrocytic endfeet are closely associated with the pia, helping limit passage between CSF and neural parenchyma.'],
['Endothelium','Maintains a nonthrombogenic interface, regulates hemostasis and inflammation, and actively transports key nutrients including glucose and amino acids.'],
['Arterial supply','Rostral, middle and caudal cerebral arteries arise from the cerebral arterial circle, associated with carotid and vertebrobasilar circulation. Vascular localization informs infarct patterns.'],
['Rete mirabile','Arterial-venous vascular network in species that possess it; serves countercurrent heat exchange relevant to cerebral temperature regulation.']
]},
{title:'Four CNS pathogen entry routes',slides:'24–25',points:[
['1. Direct extension','Penetrating trauma or spread from ear, bone or nasal cavity. Consider contiguous structures in focal cranial disease.'],
['2. Hematogenous','Most common route in the professor’s lecture: organisms arrive via the bloodstream, potentially involving meninges, parenchyma, or choroid plexus.'],
['3. Leukocyte trafficking','Macrophages or lymphoid cells transport pathogens across vascular interfaces.'],
['4. Retrograde axonal transport','Pathogens travel centripetally along nerves; lecture examples include rabies and Listeria.'],
['Clinical reasoning','Route predicts potential distribution, but an infectious diagnosis still needs organism-specific support.']
]},
{title:'Why the CNS responds poorly to injury',slides:'26–27',points:[
['High vulnerability','Neurons require continuous ATP. Severe interruption of perfusion for minutes can initiate degeneration and death.'],
['Relative ischemic susceptibility','Neurons > oligodendrocytes > astrocytes > microglia > endothelial cells (professor’s ranked order).'],
['Limits on repair','Scar-forming fibroblasts are fewer than in other organs, astrocytic scars are relatively weak, mature neuronal/tract regeneration is limited, and the cranial vault has little room for swelling.'],
['Neuronal necrosis','Ischemia, inflammation, bacterial toxins, thermal injury, heavy metals, malnutrition and trauma can cause it by damaging neurons or reducing ATP.'],
['Histology ID: red dead neurons','Necrotic neurons appear shrunken and intensely eosinophilic (pink-red) on H&E, often with nuclear injury.'],
['Mechanistic sequence','Ischemia → ATP deficit → Na+/K+-ATPase failure → loss of ion homeostasis → cellular swelling and irreversible membrane/organelle damage.']
]},
{title:'Axonal injury and Wallerian degeneration',slides:'28',points:[
['Definition','Axonal degeneration in the segment distal to an injury that interrupts the axon and its transport.'],
['Triggers','Compression, crushing, stretching, and toxic injury are examples.'],
['Microscopic features','Focal segmental axonal swellings (spheroids), disintegration of axon/myelin, and infiltrating phagocytes.'],
['Digestion chambers','Debris-clearing macrophages within a myelin tube can form digestion chambers.'],
['Differentiate','Wallerian degeneration starts with axonal injury; primary demyelination starts with direct damage to the myelin-forming cell/sheath.']
]},
{title:'Astrocyte, oligodendrocyte and ependymal responses',slides:'29–31',points:[
['Mild astrocytic response','Cell swelling, hypertrophy, limited proliferation and increased intermediate filaments/processes (reactive astrocytosis).'],
['Glial scar','Loose interconnected astrocytic processes attempt to delimit damage; not equivalent to the firm fibrous capsule of many non-CNS abscesses.'],
['Severe astrocytic injury','Processes fragment and disappear, followed by cell-body lysis.'],
['Oligodendrocyte reaction','Swelling, hypertrophy and degeneration; primary demyelination involves direct oligodendrocyte/myelin injury with a relatively intact axon (examples: ischemia, distemper, lead, autoimmune injury).'],
['Secondary demyelination','Axon injury causes subsequent loss and phagocytic removal of its myelin sheath.'],
['Ependymal injury','Atrophy, degeneration, necrosis, or ependymitis. Loss is followed by limited replacement and local astrocytosis. Ventricular expansion can compress and attenuate the lining.'],
['How ependymitis develops','Bloodborne spread via choroid plexus, direct contamination (including ruptured cerebral abscess), or reflux from leptomeningeal infection.']
]},
{title:'Microglia and meningeal injury',slides:'32–33',points:[
['Microglia early response','Often among the first cells activated. Hypertrophy, hyperplasia, phagocytosis and neuronophagia correlate with injury extent.'],
['Microglial nodules','Aggregates of activated microglia around damaged tissue.'],
['Gitter cells','Lipid/debris-laden macrophages that accumulate with marked necrosis, frequently including blood-derived monocytes.'],
['Meningitis','Inflammation of meninges, most often leptomeninges; inflammatory infiltrate accumulates chiefly in the subarachnoid space.'],
['Pachymeningitis','Inflammation of dura mater, rather than leptomeninges.'],
['Inflammation patterns','Suppurative, nonsuppurative and granulomatous; classifying cell type and location guides the differential.']
]},
{title:'Cerebral infarction and gross diagnosis',slides:'34',points:[
['Rate of ischemia matters','Abrupt vessel obstruction causes severe injury rapidly; slower flow reduction may permit collateral/anastomotic compensation.'],
['Gross appearance','Well-demarcated softened necrotic tissue (malacia). Gray matter infarcts tend to be hemorrhagic, whereas white matter infarcts often appear pale.'],
['Repair pattern','Very small lesions (<1 mm in the lecture) may be filled by astrocytosis; larger lesions may eventually form cyst-like cavities.'],
['Do not confuse','Infarction describes ischemic tissue necrosis; edema denotes excess water and may coexist with infarction, but is not equivalent to it.']
]},
{title:'Cerebral edema: gross and mechanisms',slides:'35–37',points:[
['Gross recognition','Swollen brain; narrowed or obscured sulci and flattened gyri. Increased cranial volume can produce mass effect.'],
['Cytotoxic edema','INTRACELLULAR water accumulation in neurons, astrocytes, oligodendrocytes and endothelium from failure of ATP-dependent ionic regulation. The BBB remains intact.'],
['Cytotoxic causes','Hypoxia-ischemia, severe hypothermia and selected toxicants.'],
['Vasogenic edema','EXTRACELLULAR accumulation of plasma water, ions and proteins following increased vascular permeability/blood-brain-barrier disruption.'],
['Vasogenic causes','Inflammation, contusions, hematomas, infarcts, cerebral hypertension and neoplasia. This is the most common CNS edema type according to the lecture.'],
['Critical outcome','Severe edema raises intracranial pressure, can impair neurologic function and displace brain structures caudally (e.g. cerebellar coning/herniation).'],
['Fast distinction','Cytotoxic = failed cell pumps, intact BBB, intracellular. Vasogenic = leaky BBB, extracellular, plasma fluid and protein.']
]},
{title:'Defense and barrier systems',slides:'38–42 (FYI summary)',points:[
['Blood–brain barrier (BBB)','The capillary endothelial barrier regulates passage of agents from circulating blood into brain parenchyma; specialized endothelial tight junctions are the primary seal, supported by basement membrane and astrocytic foot processes.'],
['BBB structural components','The lecture emphasizes endothelium, basement membrane and astrocytic endfeet. Specialized endothelial tight junctions limit paracellular entry; nutrient transport is selective rather than unrestricted.'],
['Glia limitans','Astrocytic processes immediately subjacent to the pia mater form a boundary associated with the pial surface, helping restrict movement from CSF-associated spaces into neural tissue. This is not the vascular BBB.'],
['Microglia and trafficking macrophages','Microglia are resident CNS innate immune cells. During inflammation, circulating monocyte-derived macrophages may enter and clear debris. Both contribute to immune responses, but neither is an anatomical barrier layer.'],
['Innate and adaptive immunity','CNS immune responses include resident innate surveillance, recruited inflammatory cells and adaptive immune-cell contributions when activated; immune defense should not be confused with BBB or glia-limitans anatomy.'],
['Clinical WHY: vasogenic edema','BBB dysfunction permits plasma fluid and proteins to enter extracellular neural tissue. Contrast cytotoxic edema, in which ionic-pump failure drives intracellular swelling with an initially intact BBB.'],
['Professor’s assessment note','The closing slide prioritizes instructor material, objectives and Canvas quizzes; the additional barrier and terminology slides are labeled FYI in the lecturer’s summary.']
],flashcards:[
['What controls movement from blood into the brain?','The blood–brain barrier, chiefly specialized capillary endothelium with tight junctions.'],
['Name the three BBB components emphasized in Lecture 67.','Endothelium, basement membrane and astrocytic foot processes.'],
['Where is the glia limitans located?','Astrocytic processes immediately beneath the pia mater.'],
['BBB versus glia limitans: which interface does each protect?','BBB: blood to neural tissue. Glia limitans: pia/CSF-associated space to neural parenchyma.'],
['Are microglia part of the anatomical BBB?','No. They are resident CNS immune sentinels and phagocytic cells.'],
['Resident microglia versus recruited macrophages?','Microglia reside in CNS; monocyte-derived macrophages can traffic in during injury or inflammation.'],
['What mechanism produces vasogenic CNS edema?','Increased BBB permeability allows extracellular accumulation of plasma fluid and proteins.']
]},
{title:'Terminology and interpretation',slides:'43 (FYI)',points:[
['Cerebral cortex','Outermost gray matter of the cerebrum, containing neuronal somata and associated neuropil/glia.'],
['Malacia','Softening of brain or spinal cord tissue, commonly resulting from necrosis and tissue breakdown, including liquefactive necrosis.'],
['Leuko- versus polio-','Leuko- identifies white matter; polio- identifies gray matter. These prefixes describe tissue distribution, not an etiologic diagnosis.'],
['Myelo- versus encephalo-','Myelo- refers to spinal cord in the context of neurologic lesion terminology; encephalo- refers to brain. Encephalomyelitis is inflammation of both brain and spinal cord.'],
['Neuronopathy','Primary process affecting neuronal cell bodies.'],
['Axonopathy','Primary process affecting axons.'],
['Myelinopathy','Primary process affecting myelin.'],
['Neuropathy','Pathology affecting nerves; may involve axons, myelin or other peripheral-nerve structures.'],
['Primary versus secondary change','An axon-first injury can lead to secondary myelin breakdown; a primary myelin lesion initially targets myelin with relative axonal preservation. Name the primary injury when evidence supports it.'],
['Look-alikes','Neuronal necrosis (“red dead”) versus axonal spheroids; primary versus secondary demyelination; cytotoxic versus vasogenic edema; meningitis versus pachymeningitis.']
],flashcards:[
['Which tissue forms the cerebral cortex?','Gray matter along the outer cerebrum.'],
['What does malacia mean in neuropathology?','Softening of brain or spinal cord tissue, often after necrosis.'],
['Leuko- versus polio-?','White matter versus gray matter.'],
['Myelo- versus encephalo-?','Spinal cord versus brain.'],
['What does encephalomyelitis involve?','Inflammation of both brain and spinal cord.'],
['A lesion starts in neuronal cell bodies: name the process.','Neuronopathy.'],
['A lesion starts in axons: name the process.','Axonopathy.'],
['A lesion primarily damages myelin: name the process.','Myelinopathy.'],
['How is neuropathy broader than axonopathy?','Neuropathy means nerve disease; axonopathy identifies primary axonal injury.'],
['Why can myelin loss follow an axonopathy?','Axonal degeneration can cause secondary breakdown of the surrounding myelin.']
]}
],
checks:[
['A dog develops acute cerebral hypoxia. Which cells are first at risk of irreversible ischemic necrosis?','Neurons. They are the highest metabolic-demand CNS cells; the lecture ranks neurons > oligodendrocytes > astrocytes > microglia > endothelium.'],
['An H&E section shows shrunken intensely eosinophilic neuronal cell bodies. What is the injury called?','Neuronal necrosis; “red dead neurons.” Do not confuse with axonal spheroids from Wallerian degeneration.'],
['A CNS lesion has swollen white matter with extracellular protein-rich fluid after a tumor disrupts vascular tight junctions. Name the edema.','Vasogenic edema: BBB breakdown → extracellular plasma fluid/protein. The professor lists this as the most common type.'],
['An animal develops neuronal ATP depletion yet its BBB remains structurally intact. Fluid builds up within cells. Name the edema.','Cytotoxic edema: ion-pump failure causes intracellular sodium accumulation and water influx.'],
['Following nerve crushing, swollen axonal segments and macrophage digestion chambers develop distal to damage. What process is occurring?','Wallerian degeneration: axonal injury disrupts axonal transport and causes distal axonal/myelin breakdown.'],
['What distinguishes primary demyelination from secondary demyelination?','Primary: injury targets oligodendrocytes/myelin while axon is relatively preserved. Secondary: axon injury precedes myelin breakdown.'],
['A culture-negative brain lesion has glial scar formation and macrophage removal of dead tissue. Which two cells have different roles?','Astrocytes produce the reactive glial scar; microglia and recruited macrophages remove debris.'],
['Trace CSF from the lateral ventricles to reabsorption.','Lateral ventricles → third → cerebral aqueduct → fourth → lateral apertures → subarachnoid space → arachnoid villi → venous circulation.'],
['Which four entry routes does the professor list for CNS pathogens?','Direct extension, hematogenous spread (most common), leukocyte trafficking, retrograde axonal transport.']
]};


window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN['68']={
title:'CNS Inflammatory Conditions',
source:'Instructor Lecture 68 · 46 slides · supplemented by Zachary & McGavin, Ch. 14',
sections:[
{title:'Objectives and inflammation patterns',slides:'1–4',points:[
['Instructor priorities','Recognize lesions and provide morphologic diagnoses; know which pathogen, route, species and characteristic lesion distinguish each CNS infection or inflammatory condition.'],
['Read the lesion first','Determine meninges, parenchyma, ventricular lining or vessel distribution. Then classify suppurative, nonsuppurative, granulomatous, necrotizing or hemorrhagic inflammation.'],
['Why location matters','Hematogenous seeding, cranial-nerve ascent, local extension and leukocyte trafficking preferentially affect different CNS compartments.']]},
{title:'Cerebral versus vertebral abscess',slides:'5',points:[
['Cerebral abscess','Can arise from direct extension or bloodborne spread, including spread associated with ventriculitis or leptomeningitis. Focal suppurative necrosis can compress vital neural structures.'],
['Weak capsule','CNS astrocytic scarring does not encapsulate purulent material as firmly as fibroblast-rich tissues; a poorly contained abscess may extend.'],
['Vertebral abscess','Adjacent osteomyelitis, discospondylitis or local trauma may produce epaxial inflammation and compress the spinal cord; hematogenous spread also occurs.'],
['What to describe','Focal or multifocal, location, purulent necrosis, displacement/compression and whether there is adjacent spinal disease.']]},
{title:'Choroid plexitis, ependymitis and meningitis',slides:'6–7',points:[
['Choroid plexitis vs ependymitis','Inflammation of choroid plexus versus ventricular ependymal lining, often bacterial and arising by bloodborne or direct entry.'],
['Gross ventricular findings','Turbid gray-white to yellow-green, thickened or gelatinous CSF; hyperemic, granular or hemorrhagic choroid plexus.'],
['Downstream complication','Inflammatory exudate may spread into subarachnoid compartments or block CSF flow and cause acquired hydrocephalus.'],
['Leptomeningitis','Inflammation of pia and arachnoid. Neutrophilic forms often follow bacteremia. Dural inflammation is pachymeningitis; viral disease often involves brain parenchyma too.']]},
{title:'Neonatal septicemia and meningitis',slides:'8–9',points:[
['Routes','Neonatal bacteremia starts from umbilical, enteric, respiratory or intrauterine entry; hematogenous dissemination seeds CNS vessels and leptomeninges.'],
['Common organisms','E. coli, Streptococcus, Salmonella, Pasteurella and Haemophilus species are emphasized in the lecture.'],
['Pathogenesis','Circulating microbial products and inflammatory mediators damage vessels and the blood-brain barrier, producing suppurative leptomeningitis with edema.'],
['Gross reasoning','Search for meningeal cloudiness/exudate and evidence of systemic neonatal infection rather than guessing the bacterium from appearance.']]},
{title:'Listeriosis',slides:'10',points:[
['Host and entry','Listeria monocytogenes is important in domestic ruminants; poor-quality silage is a major exposure.'],
['Lesion distribution','Predilection for brainstem, reflecting neural entry and ascending spread along cranial nerves; gross lesions may be absent.'],
['Histology','Characteristic neutrophilic microabscesses in the brainstem, often with perivascular inflammatory cuffs and microglial reaction.'],
['Distinguish','Focal brainstem suppurative microabscesses favor listeriosis over diffuse hematogenous bacterial meningitis.']]},
{title:'Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (TME)',slides:'11–12',points:[
['Cause and host','Histophilus somni causes septicemia and vascular injury in cattle, particularly young feedlot animals.'],
['Gross lesions','Random multifocal hemorrhagic and necrotic foci, often near the cerebral cortical gray-white junction, with edema and sometimes leptomeningitis.'],
['Mechanism','Septic vasculitis and vascular necrosis lead to thrombosis, ischemia, hemorrhage and infarction.'],
['Distinguish','The vessel-centered thrombotic/hemorrhagic lesion separates TME from Listeria brainstem microabscesses.']]},
{title:'Overeating disease and granulomatous meningoencephalitis',slides:'13–14',points:[
['Overeating disease','Clostridium perfringens type D enterotoxemia in sheep and goats after dietary change causes toxin-mediated vascular damage and BBB disruption.'],
['Typical distribution','Gross lesions often absent; when present, symmetric malacic foci, particularly in selected deep brain structures.'],
['GME','An idiopathic, suspected T-cell–mediated inflammatory disease of small-breed dogs; multifocal or focal neurologic dysfunction.'],
['Histology and distinction','Perivascular macrophage-rich/granulomatous inflammation in GME versus toxin-mediated vascular injury in overeating disease.']]},
{title:'Rabies and equine arboviruses',slides:'15–20',points:[
['Rabies entry and spread','Lyssavirus enters peripheral tissues from an infected bite, travels retrogradely in nerves and ascends into CNS; gross lesions often absent.'],
['Rabies histology','Nonsuppurative polioencephalomyelitis, microgliosis, neuron damage and characteristic Negri bodies in some neurons; absence of inclusions does not exclude infection.'],
['EEE/WEE/VEE','Mosquito-borne alphaviruses causing encephalomyelitis in equids; primarily gray matter disease with nonsuppurative inflammation and neuronal injury.'],
['West Nile virus','Mosquito-borne flavivirus with gray-matter predilection in horses and birds, often involving the spinal cord; hemorrhage may be more evident than with equine alphaviruses.'],
['Safety and clinical relevance','Suspected rabies demands appropriate public-health and laboratory precautions; histology alone is not definitive.']]},
{title:'EHV-1, MCF, CAE',slides:'21–23',points:[
['EHV-1 neurologic disease','Equine herpesvirus 1 can cause myeloencephalopathy, abortion and respiratory disease. Vascular inflammation and thrombosis produce focal ischemic necrosis in nervous tissue.'],
['Malignant catarrhal fever','Gammaherpesvirus-associated disease of susceptible ruminants targets vessels, epithelia and lymphoid tissues; systemic vasculitis with possible CNS lesions.'],
['Caprine arthritis encephalitis','Lentivirus infection of goats with macrophage tropism; young kids can develop neurologic disease while adults more often have arthritis or mammary disease.'],
['Differential','EHV-1 and MCF are prominent vascular injury syndromes; CAE has an age- and macrophage-associated disease pattern.']]},
{title:'Canine distemper, herpesvirus and feline FIP',slides:'24–27',points:[
['Canine distemper','Pantropic morbillivirus produces immunosuppression and CNS disease; viral injury to oligodendrocytes/myelin produces demyelinating encephalitis.'],
['Presentation variations','The professor differentiates classic young-dog disease, uncommon postvaccinal syndromes, and old dog encephalitis.'],
['Canine herpesvirus-1','Can produce multisystemic infection in neonates and occasionally CNS necrosis/microglial nodules, with cerebellar and brainstem involvement.'],
['FIP','Macrophage-associated feline coronavirus disease may cause pyogranulomatous/vascular CNS inflammation, ventricular exudate, nodules and secondary hydrocephalus.'],
['Compare','Distemper: demyelination; FIP: pyogranulomatous exudate and vasculitis; canine neonatal herpesvirus: systemic necrotizing infection.']]},
{title:'Fungal CNS infection and cryptococcosis',slides:'28–30',points:[
['Fungal differentials','Aspergillus, Candida, Cryptococcus, Blastomyces, Histoplasma, Coccidioides and mucoralean fungi can involve CNS.'],
['Angioinvasive fungi','Aspergillus is highlighted for vascular invasion in dogs, resulting in vasculitis, thrombosis and necrosis.'],
['Cryptococcus','C. neoformans and C. gattii may reach the CNS from nasal/sinus extension through the cribriform plate or hematogenous spread from pulmonary disease.'],
['Recognition','Soft gelatinous cryptococcal lesions, thickly encapsulated yeasts and inflammatory response that may vary by immune status; compare with angioinvasive fungal infarcts.']]},
{title:'Neospora, Toxoplasma and EPM',slides:'31–35',points:[
['Neospora caninum','Can involve neural and muscle tissues; intrauterine and ingestion routes; multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative inflammation and glial nodules.'],
['Toxoplasma gondii','Can involve numerous tissues including CNS; intracellular protozoa may produce multifocal necrotizing encephalitis.'],
['EPM','Sarcocystis neurona predominates, with Neospora hughesi another cause. The opossum is the definitive host for S. neurona; lesions can be asymmetric and randomly distributed through spinal gray/white matter.'],
['Gross and histologic clues','EPM can yield irregular dark reddish brown necrosis/hemorrhage; confirm protozoal identity with appropriate ancillary testing, not gross appearance alone.'],
['Diagnostic testing','PCR, serology/ELISA and immunohistochemistry each address different diagnostic questions; correlate with lesion location and clinical signs.']]},
{title:'Parasitic migration, Cuterebra and nematodiasis',slides:'36–40',points:[
['General CNS parasite injury','Migrating parasites cause linear hemorrhagic/necrotic tracts, malacia, space-occupying lesions and often eosinophilic inflammation.'],
['Insect larvae','Cuterebra in dogs/cats, Oestrus ovis in sheep and Hypoderma bovis in cattle are lecture examples with differing normal habitats and aberrant CNS routes.'],
['Feline ischemic encephalopathy','Aberrant Cuterebra migration can produce acute, often unilateral cerebral injury in cats, commonly in warmer months.'],
['Named nematodes','Parelaphostrongylus tenuis, Strongylus vulgaris, Toxocara canis and Baylisascaris procyonis: know host, migration route and lesions.'],
['Distinguish','Migration tracks and eosinophils versus vascular emboli; not all protozoal disease produces eosinophilic inflammation.']]},
{title:'Fibrocartilaginous embolism, IVDD and necrotizing encephalitides',slides:'41–45',points:[
['Fibrocartilaginous embolism','Disk-associated fibrocartilage enters spinal vasculature, occludes flow and produces acute ischemic myelopathy, commonly described in dogs.'],
['Intervertebral disk disease','Compression can cause direct axonal injury, Wallerian degeneration, ischemia and potentially progressive myelomalacia.'],
['Necrotizing meningoencephalitis','Small-breed dog condition historically termed pug dog encephalitis; predominantly cerebral necrosis with meningeal involvement.'],
['Necrotizing leukoencephalitis','Prominent white matter necrosis, classically described in Yorkshire terriers and other small breeds.'],
['Compare','FCE = vascular infarct; IVDD = mechanical compression plus secondary injury; NME/NLE = inflammatory necrotizing encephalitides.']]}
],
checks:[
['Why does Listeria in ruminants often target the brainstem?','Neural ascent along cranial nerves produces brainstem localization with suppurative microabscesses.'],
['A feedlot calf has randomly distributed hemorrhagic cortical junction lesions with septic thrombosis. What is the top cause?','Histophilus somni–associated thrombotic meningoencephalitis (TME).'],
['A goat with recent sudden feed change develops symmetric CNS lesions. What is the mechanism of overeating disease?','Clostridium perfringens type D epsilon toxin damages vascular endothelium/BBB, causing edema and malacia.'],
['A horse develops neurologic deficits with an asymmetric hemorrhagic spinal cord focus. Which protozoal syndrome is important?','Equine protozoal myeloencephalitis, usually Sarcocystis neurona.'],
['Which fungal genus is emphasized as angioinvasive in dogs?','Aspergillus, producing vascular damage, thrombosis and necrosis.'],
['Why can inflammatory ventricular exudate cause acquired hydrocephalus?','It blocks CSF circulation, resulting in upstream ventricular dilation.'],
['How do FCE and compressive IVDD differ mechanistically?','FCE is vascular occlusion by fibrocartilage; IVDD primarily compresses tissue and can cause subsequent ischemia and Wallerian degeneration.']
]};


window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN['69']={
title:'CNS Malformations, Neoplasia, Toxic and Degenerative Disease',
source:'Instructor Lecture 69 · 57 slides; textbook support: Zachary & McGavin, Ch. 14',
sections:[
{title:'What to recognize and how to describe it',slides:'1–4',points:[
['Professor’s objective','Recognize congenital CNS defects, gliomas and other tumors, metabolic encephalopathies, toxicoses, nutrient deficiencies, and selected spinal diseases from gross lesions and mechanisms.'],
['Gross lesion checklist','Identify involved region, symmetric or asymmetric, white or gray matter, tissue missing versus compressed, cystic versus solid, and vascular versus inflammatory injury.'],
['Distinguishing mechanism','A ventricle enlarged by accumulated CSF is not the same lesion as cerebral tissue destroyed and replaced by a fluid-filled space.']]},
{title:'Hydrocephalus and fluid pressure',slides:'5–6',points:[
['Definition','Abnormal CSF accumulation with ventricular dilation; congenital or acquired.'],
['Causes','Malformations of CSF passages, prior developmental viral injury, inherited predisposition in some toy/brachycephalic dogs, inflammation and obstruction by masses.'],
['Gross lesion','Enlarged cerebral ventricles with compression/thinning of surrounding neural tissue; congenital severe cases may have a domed, thinned skull.'],
['Why location matters','Ventricles proximal to the obstruction dilate; white matter is particularly susceptible to compression injury.'],
['Compare','Hydrocephalus expands the ventricular system, whereas hydranencephaly replaces much of the cerebral mantle after destructive fetal injury.']]},
{title:'Absent tissue versus abnormal cortical folding',slides:'7–9',points:[
['Anencephaly and cerebral aplasia','Anencephaly is extreme absence of the brain; cerebral aplasia lacks cerebral hemispheres while brainstem and cerebellum may remain.'],
['Lissencephaly','The cerebrum is abnormally smooth with reduced gyri/sulci because of abnormal neuronal migration and cortical development; breed and species matter.'],
['Porencephaly','Cystic cavity within a cerebral hemisphere, often following prenatal destructive injury.'],
['Hydranencephaly','Extensive absence of cerebral cortical and white matter tissue replaced by fluid-filled spaces, with meninges often remaining.'],
['Important sources','Fetal neurotropic viral infections may injure developing neural cells, including Akabane, BVDV and bluetongue viruses.']]},
{title:'Other CNS congenital defects',slides:'10–15',points:[
['Cerebellar hypoplasia','Underdevelopment of the cerebellum can follow viral injury to proliferating fetal or neonatal cells; affected animals have cerebellar motor signs without progressive inflammatory disease.'],
['Meningocele and meningoencephalocele','Herniation of meninges alone versus meninges plus neural tissue through a bony defect.'],
['Hydromyelia and syringomyelia','Hydromyelia dilates the central spinal canal; syringomyelia denotes an abnormal fluid-filled cavity within spinal cord parenchyma.'],
['Chiari-like malformation','Crowding/displacement of caudal brain structures can interfere with CSF flow and associate with syringomyelia.'],
['Image recognition','Assess what forms the sac/cavity, what tissue is missing, and whether the fluid-filled structure communicates with normal ventricles or central canal.']]},
{title:'Gliomas',slides:'16–20',points:[
['Three primary categories','Astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma and ependymoma are the three glial tumor types emphasized.'],
['Astrocytoma','Neoplasm of astrocytic lineage, often infiltrative and variably distinct from surrounding brain.'],
['Oligodendroglioma','Typically arises from oligodendroglial lineage and can show soft gray-pink intraparenchymal masses; histology is needed for definitive classification.'],
['Ependymoma','Arises from cells associated with ventricular or central canal lining, so location relative to CSF spaces is a major clue.'],
['Test yourself','Location, gross margins, infiltration and tissue of origin are useful, but gross color alone cannot establish the specific glial subtype.']]},
{title:'Choroid plexus tumors, meningiomas and metastasis',slides:'21–25',points:[
['Choroid plexus neoplasia','Intraventricular mass associated with CSF production pathways; may produce obstruction, hydrocephalus and pressure effects.'],
['Meningioma','Extra-axial, usually dural-associated and compressive rather than originating within the neural parenchyma.'],
['Metastatic lesions','Often multifocal, bloodborne and associated with the cerebral gray-white junction. Hemangiosarcoma is an important hemorrhagic metastatic tumor example.'],
['Gross differential','Dura-based compressive mass versus intraventricular mass versus intraparenchymal neoplasm versus multiple metastatic nodules.']]},
{title:'Hepatic encephalopathy and metabolic injury',slides:'26–30',points:[
['Mechanism','Failure of hepatic detoxification and/or portosystemic shunting allows ammonia and other neuroactive substances to affect the brain.'],
['Neural response','Hyperammonemia changes astrocyte metabolism, resulting in astrocyte swelling and functional neurologic impairment.'],
['Microscopic clue','Alzheimer type II astrocytes are a characteristic microscopic association described in pathology texts; this lecture does not require histologic identification of the lesion.'],
['Do not confuse','Metabolic dysfunction can produce marked neurologic signs without a discrete gross inflammatory or tumoral lesion.']]},
{title:'Toxic CNS injury: neurochemical versus structural lesions',slides:'30–39',points:[
['Organophosphates','Acetylcholinesterase inhibition causes cholinergic overstimulation and neuromuscular/autonomic neurologic signs; characteristic CNS gross lesions may be absent.'],
['Lead','Causes multisystemic and neurologic toxicosis, potentially including cerebral edema and neuronal degeneration; species and exposure history matter.'],
['Selenium','Toxicosis can produce focal or bilaterally symmetric spinal cord damage in susceptible species, with weakness and neurologic deficits.'],
['Salt intoxication','Sodium disturbances and correction-related osmotic shifts can contribute to encephalopathy, edema and characteristic inflammatory/neuronal lesions in pigs.'],
['Bromethalin','A mitochondrial toxicant causing CNS edema and white-matter intramyelinic vacuolation.'],
['Arsenic and tetanus','Arsenic has important vascular/GI toxicity; tetanospasmin interferes with inhibitory neurotransmitter release, causing spasticity, often without an identifiable primary gross CNS lesion.']]},
{title:'Plants, fungi, and species-specific malacia',slides:'39–45',points:[
['Phalaris grass','Neurotoxicosis can cause abnormal pigment accumulation and neurologic dysfunction, especially in grazing animals.'],
['Moldy corn (fumonisins)','Equine leukoencephalomalacia features white-matter softening/necrosis after fumonisin exposure.'],
['Centaurea species','Can produce nigropallidal encephalomalacia in horses with neurologic and feeding abnormalities.'],
['Species-location rule','Polio- means gray matter and leuko- means white matter; the lesion name indicates the preferred anatomic site.'],
['Look-alikes','Differentiate toxin-associated white/gray matter necrosis from vascular infarction and inflammatory encephalitis by distribution and exposure.']]},
{title:'Deficiency states and spinal cord compression',slides:'45–53',points:[
['Thiamine deficiency','Loss of thiamine-dependent metabolism impairs ATP production. Ruminant polioencephalomalacia and species-specific neural lesions must be interpreted in the correct metabolic context.'],
['Copper deficiency','Hypomyelination or degeneration of developing neural pathways can produce congenital or progressive motor dysfunction, especially in small ruminants.'],
['Wobbler syndrome','Cervical vertebral malformation/instability and canal narrowing can compress the spinal cord, particularly in large-breed dogs and horses.'],
['Postanesthetic myelopathy','Ischemic/spinal perfusion mechanisms are proposed in some postoperative neurologic deficits; differentiate vascular injury from mechanical compression.']]},
{title:'Pain syndromes and cognitive degeneration',slides:'54–57',points:[
['Beagle pain syndrome','Steroid-responsive meningitis-arteritis is a painful inflammatory syndrome associated with cervical pain and other signs in predisposed young dogs.'],
['Canine cognitive dysfunction','Age-associated neurodegeneration causes behavioral/cognitive decline, potentially without a distinctive gross lesion.'],
['Final recognition rule','Work from the clinical presentation and the lesion distribution to the mechanism. A named disease is a differential until the pattern and testing support it.']]}
],
checks:[
['Which gross lesion suggests hydrocephalus instead of hydranencephaly?','Expanded normal ventricular spaces with compression and thinning of the cerebral mantle, rather than extensive absence of cerebral hemispheric tissue.'],
['A cerebral mass is attached to dura and compresses the underlying cortex. Which primary tumor category is favored?','Meningioma, an extra-axial dural-based tumor.'],
['What separates hydromyelia and syringomyelia?','Hydromyelia is dilation of the spinal central canal; syringomyelia is a cavity within spinal cord parenchyma.'],
['A horse exposed to moldy corn has white-matter malacia. What toxin-related process is important?','Fumonisin-associated equine leukoencephalomalacia.'],
['What is the important neurotoxin in hepatic encephalopathy?','Ammonia, among other compounds, alters astrocyte metabolism and brain function.'],
['A canine lesion lies adjacent to a ventricle and blocks CSF drainage. What is the major secondary consequence?','Obstructive/acquired hydrocephalus and increased intracranial pressure.']
]};


window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN['70']={
title:'Peripheral Nervous System: Function and Pathology',
source:'Instructor Lecture 70 · 30 slides; supported by Zachary & McGavin, Ch. 14',
sections:[
{title:'Lecture objectives and CNS/PNS distinction',slides:'1–5',points:[
['Professor priorities','Explain PNS organization, somatic versus autonomic function, nerve cells and axonal transport, injury/repair, toxic, nutritional, congenital and species-specific peripheral neuropathies.'],
['Scope','Peripheral nerves connect CNS to effectors and sensory receptors; somatic nerves mediate voluntary muscle activity and autonomic pathways govern involuntary responses.'],
['Critical anatomic exception','Cranial nerves I and II are considered CNS-associated pathways with oligodendrocyte rather than Schwann-cell myelin.']]},
{title:'Somatic and autonomic components',slides:'6–7',points:[
['Somatic pathways','Afferent fibers carry sensory information to CNS; efferent motor fibers carry signals to skeletal muscle at the neuromuscular junction.'],
['Autonomic pathways','Sympathetic prepares the body for action, parasympathetic supports rest/digestion, enteric neurons coordinate GI smooth muscle, secretion and absorption.'],
['Enteric plexuses','Myenteric plexus is closely linked to intestinal motility, and submucosal plexus to secretion, absorption and local blood flow.'],
['Clinical connection','Motor neuropathy may produce weakness/denervation; autonomic neuropathy may cause ileus, dysphagia, abnormal secretions or cardiovascular dysfunction.']]},
{title:'Axon physiology and peripheral nerve structure',slides:'8–9',points:[
['Myelinated versus unmyelinated','Myelinated axons conduct more rapidly; unmyelinated fibers conduct more slowly and are important in autonomic and sensory signaling.'],
['Axoplasmic transport','Microtubules support anterograde movement from soma to terminals and retrograde movement back to soma; neurofilaments provide structural integrity.'],
['Nerve sheaths','Endoneurium surrounds individual fibers, perineurium surrounds fascicles, epineurium surrounds the entire nerve.'],
['Schwann cells','Form PNS myelin and support regeneration, unlike CNS oligodendrocytes. Satellite glial cells surround ganglion neurons.'],
['Repair physiology','Schwann cell support, vascular supply and preserved endoneurial tubes facilitate some PNS regrowth, but severe transection or long gaps limit recovery.']]},
{title:'Dysautonomia and enteric syndromes',slides:'10–14',points:[
['Dysautonomia','Degeneration or dysfunction of autonomic ganglion neurons affects multiple organ systems: GI movement, pupils, mucous membranes and cardiovascular regulation.'],
['Equine colonic aganglionosis','Loss of enteric neurons can result in failure of normal colonic motility and intestinal dysfunction.'],
['Peritonitis-associated autonomic dysfunction','Inflammation around intestinal structures can disrupt enteric neural signaling and contribute to paralytic ileus.'],
['Pattern recognition','The clinical distribution reflects loss of coordinated autonomic output rather than necessarily a visible gross peripheral nerve lesion.']]},
{title:'Myelination, endocrine and nutritional disorders',slides:'14–19',points:[
['Congenital hypomyelinating polyneuropathy','Reduced myelin development in predisposed young dogs, including golden retrievers in this lecture, produces weakness and neurologic deficits.'],
['Coyotillo toxicosis','Plant-associated myelin/nerve injury producing neurologic dysfunction, particularly in grazing animals.'],
['Endocrine disease','Endocrine and metabolic disturbances can damage nerve fibers or impair nerve metabolism, producing peripheral neuropathy.'],
['Nutrition','The lecture includes vitamin A and riboflavin deficiency as examples of nutrition-linked peripheral nervous dysfunction.'],
['Differentiate','Hypomyelination means myelin was inadequately formed; demyelination means previously formed myelin has been lost.']]},
{title:'Botulism',slides:'19–22',points:[
['Mechanism','Clostridium botulinum toxin prevents synaptic acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction by disrupting vesicle fusion proteins.'],
['Consequence','Flaccid muscle paralysis, reduced tone and weakness without an obligate gross lesion in the muscle or peripheral nerve.'],
['Species context','Recognize predisposing exposure history and syndromes in birds, horses, cattle and other susceptible species.'],
['Compare with tetanus','Botulism prevents acetylcholine release at motor terminals, producing flaccid paralysis; tetanus inhibits inhibitory neurotransmission in CNS pathways, producing spastic paralysis.']]},
{title:'Traumatic injury and peripheral neoplasms',slides:'22–25',points:[
['Neurapraxia','Transient conduction block without complete axonal discontinuity; recovery may be possible when the cause is relieved.'],
['Axonotmesis','Axonal interruption with some supporting connective tissue preserved, producing Wallerian degeneration distal to lesion.'],
['Neurotmesis','Complete severing of nerve and supporting structures, with poor spontaneous recovery.'],
['Schwannoma','Neoplasm of Schwann-cell lineage, often a firm nerve-associated mass; histology distinguishes it from other spindle-cell tumors.'],
['Why severity matters','Whether an endoneurial pathway remains intact helps predict axonal regeneration and functional recovery.']]},
{title:'Species-specific peripheral nerve diseases',slides:'25–30',points:[
['Equine grass sickness','Dysautonomia with degeneration of autonomic and enteric neurons causing severe ileus and related dysfunction; gross neural lesions may be subtle.'],
['Equine recurrent laryngeal neuropathy','Long axon degeneration, commonly left-sided, results in cricoarytenoideus dorsalis denervation atrophy and impaired arytenoid abduction.'],
['Canine acute idiopathic polyradiculoneuritis','Acute weakness reflecting inflammation of motor nerve roots with flaccid lower motor neuron signs.'],
['English pointer sensory neuropathy','Inherited sensory neuropathy resulting in poor sensation and potential self-injury.'],
['Final differentiation','Localize to sensory, motor or autonomic pathways, identify myelin versus axonal injury, and anticipate denervation changes in target muscle.']]}
],
checks:[
['What makes cranial nerves I and II unusual relative to most peripheral nerves?','They are CNS-associated and myelinated by oligodendrocytes rather than Schwann cells.'],
['What is the difference between neurapraxia and neurotmesis?','Neurapraxia is a transient conduction block; neurotmesis is complete disruption of the nerve and its supporting tissues.'],
['A horse with left arytenoid paralysis has atrophy of cricoarytenoideus dorsalis. Which mechanism?','Left recurrent laryngeal neuropathy causing denervation atrophy.'],
['Which toxin produces flaccid paralysis by preventing ACh release?','Botulinum neurotoxin at the neuromuscular junction.'],
['Why can PNS axons regenerate more effectively than CNS axons?','Schwann cells, endoneurial guidance tubes and more efficient debris cleanup facilitate regeneration when structural continuity is preserved.']
]};

window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN['71']={"title":"Dermatopathology 1: Structure, Reaction Patterns and Endocrine Skin Disease","source":"Instructor Lecture 71 · 60 slides; textbook supplement: Zachary & McGavin","sections":[{"title":"Normal skin and basement membrane","slides":"4–9","points":[["Core concept 1","Epidermis: keratinizing stratified squamous epithelium, melanocytes and immune cells; understand basal proliferation and desquamation."],["Core concept 2","Dermis: collagen, elastin, proteoglycans, vessels, nerves and adnexa. Subcutis: adipose and fascia."],["Core concept 3","Basement membrane anchors epidermis to dermis via hemidesmosomes and collagen VII anchoring fibrils; important for epithelial neoplasia and blistering diseases."]]},{"title":"Read reaction patterns before a disease name","slides":"10–12","points":[["Core concept 1","Identify gross atrophy, thickening, masses, ulceration/erosion and changes in pigment."],["Core concept 2","Microscopic reaction patterns include perivascular/interstitial, interface/cytotoxic, vesiculopustular, vasculitis, nodular/diffuse, follicular and panniculitis."],["Core concept 3","Several microscopic reaction patterns may coexist; descriptive diagnosis should reflect distribution, depth and process."]]},{"title":"Atrophy and thickened skin","slides":"13–17","points":[["Core concept 1","Atrophy is thinning due to diminished epidermal/dermal maintenance: glucocorticoids, ischemia, malnutrition, irradiation and age can contribute."],["Core concept 2","Lichenification is leathery thickening with accentuated skin markings from chronic inflammation/friction."],["Core concept 3","Hyperkeratosis expands the stratum corneum; distinguish thickened keratin from epidermal hyperplasia and dermal fibrosis."]]},{"title":"Primary and secondary lesions","slides":"18–27","points":[["Core concept 1","Define papule, plaque, pustule, vesicle, bulla, nodule, cyst, comedone, crust, collarette and callus by tissue layer and contents."],["Core concept 2","Erosion is superficial epidermal loss that leaves basement membrane relatively intact; ulceration breaches into deeper tissue and may scar."],["Core concept 3","Distinguish localized collections and proliferations from trauma-induced changes or chronic inflammation."]]},{"title":"Pigmentation and hair disease","slides":"28–35","points":[["Core concept 1","Alopecia can arise from altered follicle cycling, inflammation, parasites, endocrine disruption or physical removal of hair."],["Core concept 2","Hyperpigmentation, depigmentation, leukotrichia and pigmentation changes require epidermal versus follicular localization."],["Core concept 3","Distinguish inflammatory pruritic hair loss from typically more symmetric minimally inflammatory endocrine patterns."]]},{"title":"Endocrine dermatoses","slides":"36–48","points":[["Core concept 1","Hyperadrenocorticism can cause thin fragile skin, comedones, bilaterally symmetric alopecia and impaired wound repair."],["Core concept 2","Hypothyroidism, sex hormone imbalances and pituitary disorders alter follicular cycling, skin texture and pigmentation."],["Core concept 3","Equine PPID and species-specific endocrine presentations should not be reduced to a single canine pattern."]]},{"title":"Nutritional and metabolic disorders","slides":"49–60","points":[["Core concept 1","Zinc-responsive dermatosis and defects of keratinization often cause hyperkeratotic crusting at characteristic sites."],["Core concept 2","Hepatocutaneous syndrome produces distinctive skin lesions associated with severe metabolic liver disease."],["Core concept 3","Use lesion distribution, species, history and systemic findings to distinguish metabolic conditions from infectious dermatitis."]]}],"checks":[["What does the professor expect on an image-identification question?","First describe lesion location, morphology and distribution; then interpret likely mechanism and etiologic differential."],["What source has priority for this lecture?","The original instructor slide sequence and figures, followed by supporting textbook explanations."]]};
window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN['72']={"title":"Dermatopathology 2: Viral and Bacterial Skin Diseases","source":"Instructor Lecture 72 · 49 slides; textbook supplement: Zachary & McGavin","sections":[{"title":"The professor’s image-diagnosis framework","slides":"1–3","points":[["Core concept 1","For each infection identify organism, species, route, hallmark lesion and pathogenesis."],["Core concept 2","Offer both morphologic diagnosis (what the lesion is) and etiologic diagnosis (agent), but do not infer an organism without supporting context."]]},{"title":"Poxvirus families and transmission","slides":"4–6","points":[["Core concept 1","Poxvirus lesions may follow direct contact, contaminated wounds or arthropod transfer; species specificity and zoonotic potential differ."],["Core concept 2","Separate capripoxvirus (sheeppox, goatpox, lumpy skin disease), orthopoxvirus (cowpox), parapoxvirus (orf, bovine papular stomatitis, pseudocowpox), avipoxvirus and suipoxvirus."]]},{"title":"Sheeppox, goatpox, cowpox and fowlpox","slides":"7–10","points":[["Core concept 1","Sheeppox can cause widespread severe systemic disease with firm crusted papules, respiratory or visceral lesions."],["Core concept 2","Goatpox can produce similar cutaneous patterns; distribution and clinical severity vary."],["Core concept 3","Cowpox can involve cattle teats and felid face/limbs; has zoonotic significance."],["Core concept 4","Fowlpox has dry cutaneous lesions on non-feathered skin and wet diphtheritic mucosal lesions."]]},{"title":"Parapoxvirus diseases and oral differentials","slides":"11–16","points":[["Core concept 1","Orf affects young sheep/goats, producing proliferative crusting lesions at the muzzle and oral margins; enter through injured skin and can be zoonotic."],["Core concept 2","Bovine papular stomatitis and pseudocowpox cause characteristic cattle oral or teat lesions and can resemble other vesicular/proliferative infections."],["Core concept 3","Separate high-consequence reportable vesicular diseases before assuming a benign parapoxvirus."]]},{"title":"Papillomaviruses, herpesviruses and emerging viral causes","slides":"17–24","points":[["Core concept 1","Papillomatous lesions are proliferative epithelial growths; papillomavirus infection can be species and site specific."],["Core concept 2","Equine sarcoids have distinct biologic behavior despite association with bovine papillomavirus."],["Core concept 3","Herpesviruses produce epithelial necrosis/ulcers; distinguish genital, facial and cutaneous variants by agent and host."]]},{"title":"Superficial bacterial diseases","slides":"25–32","points":[["Core concept 1","Superficial pyoderma and folliculitis involve epidermis/follicles; pustules and epidermal collarettes are important morphologic clues."],["Core concept 2","Intertrigo occurs in folds due to moisture/friction and microbial overgrowth; distinguish from primary systemic infection."],["Core concept 3","Dermatophilosis produces characteristic adherent crusts with branching bacterial filaments."]]},{"title":"Deep bacterial disease and destructive inflammation","slides":"33–41","points":[["Core concept 1","Furunculosis follows rupture of follicles and keratin release into dermis, producing deep pyogranulomatous inflammation."],["Core concept 2","Cellulitis and necrotizing infection extend into dermis/subcutis and can have systemic consequences."],["Core concept 3","Recognize organism-associated diseases such as erysipelas, exudative epidermitis, anthrax and foot infections in their correct hosts."]]},{"title":"Hoof and species-specific lesions","slides":"42–49","points":[["Core concept 1","Equine canker is proliferative tissue growth; thrush is destructive infection of frog tissue—do not swap them."],["Core concept 2","Reportable/zoonotic considerations matter when interpreting livestock lesions."],["Core concept 3","Use gross photographs to separate papular, pustular, ulcerative, crusting, necrotizing and proliferative morphologies."]]}],"checks":[["What does the professor expect on an image-identification question?","First describe lesion location, morphology and distribution; then interpret likely mechanism and etiologic differential."],["What source has priority for this lecture?","The original instructor slide sequence and figures, followed by supporting textbook explanations."]]};
window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN['73']={"title":"Dermatopathology 3: Parasitic, Fungal and Nutritional Diseases","source":"Instructor Lecture 73 · 40 slides; textbook supplement: Zachary & McGavin","sections":[{"title":"The skin parasite framework","slides":"1–4","points":[["Core concept 1","Know each agent’s host, microhabitat (surface, stratum corneum, follicle or deeper tissue), route and resulting lesion."],["Core concept 2","Ectoparasite infestation differs from internal parasite infection with dermal migration."]]},{"title":"Demodicosis across species","slides":"5–7","points":[["Core concept 1","Canine Demodex generally occupies follicles; loss of host-parasite balance can cause alopecia, comedones, scale, folliculitis and furunculosis."],["Core concept 2","Feline D. cati is follicular/sebaceous; D. gatoi is superficial and contagious, with pruritic overgrooming."],["Core concept 3","Large-animal demodicosis has species-specific lesions; in cattle and pigs consider hides and nodular skin disease."]]},{"title":"Scabies and other mites","slides":"8–14","points":[["Core concept 1","Sarcoptes burrows superficially, is contagious and zoonotic, and causes intense pruritus, papules, excoriations and crusts."],["Core concept 2","Distinguish Notoedres, Otodectes, Psoroptes, Chorioptes and Cheyletiella by host, tissue habitat, lesion site and transmission."],["Core concept 3","A negative scraping does not rule out every mite infestation; consider lesion pattern and appropriate diagnostic techniques."]]},{"title":"Fleas, lice, ticks and myiasis","slides":"15–21","points":[["Core concept 1","Parasite feeding can cause direct trauma, pruritus and hypersensitivity; species and anatomic distribution matter."],["Core concept 2","Myiasis can produce cavitary wounds and necrosis; distinguish facultative wound infestation from obligatory tissue invasion."]]},{"title":"Parasitic migration and cutaneous nodules","slides":"22–27","points":[["Core concept 1","Habronema, Onchocerca and Stephanofilaria can cause focal granulomatous/eosinophilic lesions and draining tracts in particular species."],["Core concept 2","Identify parasite-related migratory lesions by typical site, eosinophilia and environmental exposure."]]},{"title":"Dermatophytosis and Malassezia","slides":"28–32","points":[["Core concept 1","Dermatophytes invade keratinized hair and stratum corneum; circular alopecia, broken hairs and scale are important clues but not diagnostic alone."],["Core concept 2","Malassezia proliferation often accompanies underlying skin disease and causes greasy, malodorous dermatitis."],["Core concept 3","Contrast fungal dermatophytosis with bacterial Dermatophilus infection—similar names, different organisms and lesions."]]},{"title":"Deep fungal, oomycete and nutritional lesions","slides":"33–40","points":[["Core concept 1","Pythiosis and systemic/deep fungal infections can form nodules, ulcers and draining tracts; histologic organisms and tissue response determine definitive classification."],["Core concept 2","Zinc, copper and other micronutrient disturbances may generate hyperkeratosis, coat changes or pigment abnormalities."],["Core concept 3","Pair gross distribution with organism morphology and species when forming a differential."]]}],"checks":[["What does the professor expect on an image-identification question?","First describe lesion location, morphology and distribution; then interpret likely mechanism and etiologic differential."],["What source has priority for this lecture?","The original instructor slide sequence and figures, followed by supporting textbook explanations."]]};
window.VPQ_PATH_LECTURE_LEARN['74']={"title":"Dermatopathology 4: Immunologic and Hypersensitivity Diseases","source":"Instructor Lecture 74 · 54 slides; textbook supplement: Zachary & McGavin","sections":[{"title":"Immunologic lesion recognition","slides":"1–3","points":[["Core concept 1","Determine if the immune attack is vascular, epidermal, basement membrane or deeper connective tissue."],["Core concept 2","Know etiology, species, lesion pattern, pathogenesis and recognizable gross features for every listed disease."]]},{"title":"Cutaneous vasculitis","slides":"4–6","points":[["Core concept 1","Type III immune-complex mechanisms are important in many animal vasculitides, though causes can remain undetermined."],["Core concept 2","Acute vascular injury creates purpura, hemorrhagic bullae and edema; chronic ischemia produces sharply demarcated necrosis and crusted ulcers."],["Core concept 3","Assess vessel size, lesion distribution, immune or infectious triggers and systemic disease."]]},{"title":"Subepidermal blistering and bullous disease","slides":"7–14","points":[["Core concept 1","Autoantibodies to components of the dermal-epidermal junction cause separation below the epidermis."],["Core concept 2","Compare bullous pemphigoid and epidermolysis bullosa with intraepidermal pemphigus lesions by anatomical level of cleavage."],["Core concept 3","Intact vesicles may rupture rapidly, leaving erosions and ulcers; histology is crucial for depth."]]},{"title":"Pemphigus and epidermal acantholysis","slides":"15–22","points":[["Core concept 1","Pemphigus foliaceus forms superficial/subcorneal pustules with acantholytic keratinocytes, often crusted on pinnae, face and footpads."],["Core concept 2","Pemphigus vulgaris has deeper suprabasal separation and more severe mucocutaneous erosions."],["Core concept 3","Acantholysis can occasionally accompany other disease; pattern plus immunologic testing establish etiology."]]},{"title":"Lupus, VKH and pigment-related immune disease","slides":"23–31","points":[["Core concept 1","Cutaneous lupus can produce depigmentation, scaling and erosive lesions at characteristic mucocutaneous sites."],["Core concept 2","Uveodermatologic syndrome (VKH-like disease) involves immune attack on melanocytes and links ocular inflammation with skin depigmentation."],["Core concept 3","Dermatomyositis and vitiligo have differing histologic and clinical patterns."]]},{"title":"Hypersensitivity patterns","slides":"32–41","points":[["Core concept 1","Type I hypersensitivity promotes pruritus, urticaria and angioedema; chronic self-trauma can cause secondary skin changes."],["Core concept 2","Atopic, flea, food-related and contact conditions have overlapping signs; distinguish by distribution, exposure and diagnostic method."],["Core concept 3","Culicoides hypersensitivity and mosquito bite hypersensitivity have species/site-specific clues."]]},{"title":"Eosinophilic lesions and severe mucocutaneous reactions","slides":"42–49","points":[["Core concept 1","The feline eosinophilic granuloma complex includes plaques, indolent ulcers and linear granulomas with different characteristic sites."],["Core concept 2","Erythema multiforme, SJS and toxic epidermal necrolysis differ in mechanism and severity and require careful clinical interpretation."]]},{"title":"Hereditary and secondary response patterns","slides":"50–54","points":[["Core concept 1","Inherited keratinization and connective tissue disorders may mimic acquired inflammation."],["Core concept 2","Always distinguish primary disease from secondary infection, scratching or chronic fibrosis."]]}],"checks":[["What does the professor expect on an image-identification question?","First describe lesion location, morphology and distribution; then interpret likely mechanism and etiologic differential."],["What source has priority for this lecture?","The original instructor slide sequence and figures, followed by supporting textbook explanations."]]};
